#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203770 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc557b-f9f5-5dfc-a860-a47b76b4fc42" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/id #uuid "37f0d2c0-c174-5478-a007-5fb3d32bafe2" :question/math-academy-id "e-16569" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,2] \\times [0,2] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2}y - x - 2 \\\\ xy^{2} - y + 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 2 \\big]^T$." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $\\boldsymbol{f}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nwhere $J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian of $\\boldsymbol f$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n$.\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ_{f}(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}y - x - 2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2}y - x - 2) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy^{2} - y + 1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy^{2} - y + 1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2xy - 1 & x^{2} \\\\ y^{2} & 2xy - 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2xy - 1 & x^{2} \\\\ y^{2} & 2xy - 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nStarting with $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} - \\begin{bmatrix}2xy - 1 & x^{2} \\\\ y^{2} & 2xy - 1\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2}y - x - 2 \\\\ xy^{2} - y + 1\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}2(1)(2) - 1 & (1)^{2} \\\\ (2)^{2} & 2(1)(2) - 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}(1)^{2}(2) - 1 - 2 \\\\ 1(2)^{2} - 2 + 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{1}{3 \\cdot 3 - 4 \\cdot 1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{11}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2.2 \\\\ -0.6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203771 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bad338b3-41cf-5193-aef7-d73c8f859c69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/id #uuid "1fa3ed18-5fb9-53b2-a067-5db623a289d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-16570" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y)$ in the region $[1,3] \\times [-2,0] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} + y - 4 \\\\ x + y^{2} - 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 0 \\big]^T$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives $\\mathbf{x}_1 = \\big[3, \\: -1 \\big]^T$. Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed)." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $\\boldsymbol{f}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nwhere $J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian of $\\boldsymbol f$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n$.\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ_{f}(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + y - 4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + y - 4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x + y^{2} - 3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x + y^{2} - 3)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} - \\begin{bmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{1}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} + y - 4 \\\\ x + y^{2} - 3\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}2(3) & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2(-1)\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3^{2} + (-1) - 4 \\\\ 3 + (-1)^{2} - 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} - (1)/(6 \\cdot (-2) - 1 \\cdot 1)\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} + \\frac{1}{13}\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{30}{13} \\\\ -\\frac{11}{13}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&\\approx \\begin{bmatrix}2.308 \\\\ -0.846\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203772 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12c74b6-ac99-514f-96c4-a26e8fef6028" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/id #uuid "5da362d4-cb58-5b51-acc0-582e3a0d42e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-16571" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $\\boldsymbol{f}(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1] \\times [0,1] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} - y^{2} \\\\ 2xy - 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the matrix equation that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $\\big[1, \\: 1 \\big]^T$, and compute the components of the first iteration." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $\\boldsymbol{f}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^n$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nwhere $J_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ is the Jacobian of $\\boldsymbol f$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n$.\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as\n\n$$\nJ_\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1}-\\mathbf{x}_n) = -\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ_{f}(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} - y^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} - y^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy - 1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy - 1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2x & -2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x & -2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nStarting with $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x & -2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}}(x_{1} - x_{0}) &= -\\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} - y^{2} \\\\ 2xy - 1\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}2(1) & -2(1) \\\\ 2(1) & 2(1)\\end{bmatrix}(x_{1} - x_{0}) &= -\\begin{bmatrix}(1)^{2} - (1)^{2} \\\\ 2(1)(1) - 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}(x_{1} - x_{0}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $\\begin{bmatrix}-0.25 \\\\ -0.25\\end{bmatrix}$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} + \\begin{bmatrix}-0.25 \\\\ -0.25\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-0.25 \\\\ -0.25\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.75 \\\\ 0.75\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12c74b6-ac99-514f-96c4-a26e8fef6028" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "02634e23-5ac7-5178-b75a-318bb2114e67" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202923} {:answer-field/id #uuid "0ffcef57-8caf-55fd-8416-f05f2a94349b" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202924} {:answer-field/id #uuid "2e2b4345-6b1c-5f68-ad87-cbe1cfa8cf80" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202925} {:answer-field/id #uuid "75d5768d-e966-550e-9852-db775eb656c4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202926}] :question/id #uuid "76b77696-fb71-5bf3-bbfc-fa9d52e3daf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-255856" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[0, 1] \\cdot [0, 1]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = \\begin{bmatrix}2x^{2} + y^{2} - 2 \\\\ x^{3} + y^{3} - 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[1, 1]^{T}$, and compute the components of the first iteration, rounding your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).$[MATH: []$\n{{field-1}}\n$2$$[MATH:](x_{1} - x_{0}) = []$\n{{field-2}}\n$[MATH:]]$\n{{field-3}}\n$3$$-1$\n$[MATH: x_{1} \\approx []$$0.833$$[MATH:]]$\n{{field-4}}\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"derivation-of-the-formula\"></a>"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc557b-f9f5-5dfc-a860-a47b76b4fc42" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "eff474b0-18cb-5c4d-95e0-0d757eb60a97" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202954}] :question/id #uuid "83394f73-0e0c-509d-afc6-c9fbe175f1ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-256200" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[- 2, 0] \\cdot [- 2, 0]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = \\begin{bmatrix}xy - 1 \\\\ y^{3} + 3xy^{4} + 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with $x_{0} = [- 1,-1]^{T}$. Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).$[MATH: x_{1} \\approx []$\n{{field-1}}\n$[MATH:]]$$-0.917$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bad338b3-41cf-5193-aef7-d73c8f859c69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "b813800e-299c-572c-8296-6376424a1e38" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202956} {:answer-field/id #uuid "4d6fda47-383d-5900-a32d-d49a762ab08a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202957}] :question/id #uuid "d0cfa679-aa5b-51c4-931a-565874b7b55c" :question/math-academy-id "q-256229" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[1, 2] \\cdot [0, 1]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = \\begin{bmatrix}x^{2} + y - xy - 3 \\\\ x^{2} - x + y - 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at $x_{0} = [1, 0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives $x_{1} = [2, 1]^{T}$. Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).$[MATH: x_{2} \\approx []$\n{{field-1}}\n$[MATH:]]$\n{{field-2}}\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"alternative-to-newtons-method-for-multivariable-functions-using-linear-systems\"></a>"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12c74b6-ac99-514f-96c4-a26e8fef6028" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "caada121-cce4-5288-abae-914850348617" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202964} {:answer-field/id #uuid "fcffd96b-b05a-516f-8e29-0e2910018bc6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202965} {:answer-field/id #uuid "2d7abab1-331d-535f-ba33-60f6fed123a4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186202966}] :question/id #uuid "d65c1411-1261-569b-b79c-90235ce25c18" :question/math-academy-id "q-256287" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[0, 1] \\cdot [1, 2]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = \\begin{bmatrix}x^{3}y - 5y + 8 \\\\ xy^{2} - 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[1, 1]^{T}$.$[MATH: []$$3$$-4$$[MATH:](x_{1} - x_{0}) = []$\n{{field-1}}\n$[MATH:].]$\n{{field-2}}\n\n{{field-3}}\n$2$"}]}
